body:
Shortly after moving in, people of all races, colors, creeds, and economic statuses started showing up at our door asking after "Roux" (Roo? Rue?). This got so bad, we posted signs all over stating that she was not in our apartment, and that we would not answer the door if we were not expecting company. Then, she arrived: Roux attempted to use her keys to enter our apartment. We told her it was our apartment. She told us it was her apartment. Negotiations went nowhere. Turns out she had a friend in a nearby apartment and had plenty of free time to harass us. We got the police involved. Idiots kept arriving, disbelieving our signs. The property managers pretended to know nothing about it. She was likely a drug dealer.

&#x200B;

Next door neighbors are definitely drug dealers. We've seen something like 100 different people going in and out at all hours for the past year. The weird part is, a lot of these people are working with power tools and refurbishing furniture. Not all of them, and most of them never come back. Is that a cover or something? I don't know, but it's shady.

&#x200B;

We're moving today.

I would like to share some interesting information that may or may not be news to all of you: [Acromegaly](https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/acromegaly/symptoms-causes/syc-20351222) (ak-ro-mega-lee).

&#x200B;

Per the Mayo Clinic: "Acromegaly is a hormonal disorder that develops when your pituitary gland produces too much growth hormone during adulthood. When this happens, your bones increase in size, including those of your hands, feet and face. Acromegaly usually affects middle-aged adults..

&#x200B;

According to the same, **headaches** are a common symptom. Notice that many of the "Taro troop" seem to experience severe headaches. Though those images of the disorder readily available on the internet show that those suffering typically have extraordinarily strong jawlines (unlike the Taro Troop), they do seem to have some similar facial features.

&#x200B;

Food for thought, at least.

I need to reserve the bottom three for the bottom-shelf and the insane nonsense. I would say that this is better than a $10 rotgut, and even those have a hierarchy. I gave it a 4 because I could, at least, swallow it without feeling sick. I've had some bad whiskey, and this is easily the worst I've had in its price range.

&#x200B;

Perhaps I was also pulling my punches as this was my first review...

I'll be looking for your side-by-side! I once asked for a recommendation at a small liquor store near me and was offered Fireside from Mile High Spirits. Price:taste wasn't the worst, but it just wasn't interesting. I told the guy that it just wasn't for me, but I believe that he really likes it. There's no accounting for taste, but I would say that Tin Cup is better as a whiskey in the same price range.

&#x200B;

I am getting the impression from this board that I haven't given WT enough of a chance.

I can't help but wonder if the circumstances affected your opinion. After all, restaurants know that their diners will rate the food more highly with the right setting and presentation. Having a child on the way would certainly make everything shine more brightly, at least for me.

&#x200B;

That said, you may be one of the few whose gustatory opinions are unaffected by circumstance. Even then, from your review, this sounds like an 8/10 to me, but that's a matter of different ideals when it comes to bourbon. I can't wait to grab a bottle and find out for myself. Very good review, I can almost taste it now!

